# pyenv
#   See https://github.com/pyenv/pyenv/wiki/Using-pyenv-as-a-deployable-tool
#   for more information on how to manage multiple Python versions
#   with pyenv. If you want pyenv to be found automatically, add the
#   following line to your ~/.bashrc (or ~/.bash_profile):
#   export PYENV_ROOT="$HOME/.pyenv"
#   export PATH="$PYENV_ROOT/bin:$PATH"
#
#   Then, you should be able to run `pyenv` and `pyenv install` and `pyenv use`
#   from your shell.
#
#   If any of those commands don't work, see https://github.com/pyenv/pyenv/issues/76
#   for some known issues.
#
#   This directory will be ignored by git, but it will be checked by
#   pyenv. If you are using a version of pyenv that doesn't check this
#   directory, you may need to remove the following line from your pyenv
#   configuration (see https://github.com/pyenv/pyenv/wiki/Using-pyenv-as-a-deployable-tool):
#   export PYENV_ROOT_PATH="$HOME/.pyenv"

